WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
What should you look for in a focus group
moderator?

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.700
Think of a moderator as a guide on a trail
your research team has already

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.200
mapped. The guide keeps the group moving,
but also notices the unexpected

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.900
turn worth exploring.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.580
Start with the decision your study must
inform.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.620
Then define the audience, discussion
guide, session format

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.360
and who owns recruitment,

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.420
consent, notes and analysis.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.980
A moderator can lead a group or an
individual interview,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.360
but those responsibilities should be
agreed before fieldwork.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.480
For example, a research firm may have its
sample and analysis plan ready but

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.160
need an experienced person to lead six
conversations.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.480
That calls for a defined moderation role.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.920
If the question and method are still open,
the team may need a fuller

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:50.920
qualitative study.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.560
PRC Impact provides RIVA-trained
moderation and research support.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.280
Tell us what your team has in place and
what it needs next at

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.460
prcimpact.com, or call

